          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "Wrapped up. Since I worked in a fresh clone of the GitHub repo rather than your local checkout, the changes are delivered as a patch (apply from the repo root with `git apply wilson-lines-session2.patch`) and as a tarball of the same eight files.\n\n**What the session did.** I read the handoff, both endpoint notes, the review, Loewner §4, the manuscript's conventions and generator, and Baker 1102.4948 in full, verified the existing replays, then did the handoff's head item — construct the positive pairing — and wrote it up as `notes/REFLECTION_NETWORKS_AND_THE_EVEN_TOWER_20260918.md` with a standard-library check programme (1196 deterministic cases, record saved). Manuscript untouched at 0.8.\n\n**The result, briefly.** The archimedean kernel splits by reflection: $n_\\gamma = G_o + G_-$ exactly. Reflecting *within* the defect ($x_1\\to-x_1$) with the origin as reference glues only the alternating tower $1/(2\\cosh(u/2))$, with a backtracking cusp at the origin and no common supercharge — that is the version of Codex's pairwise obstruction that survives. Reflecting *through* the defect ($x_3\\to-x_3$), with kets $\\Phi_r = W_{\\rm up}[0\\leftarrow r]\\,q(r)$ along the semicircle of diameter $[0,r]$ and the even combination $\\Phi_r+\\Phi_{-r}$, gives a gauge-invariant open Wilson line through the origin, smooth there, whose free kernel is exactly $n_\\gamma$, and which is a Gram kernel **conditional on** OS positivity of the interface theory under that reflection. All kets for all $r$ share one bulk special supercharge ($\\epsilon_s=0$, $J\\epsilon_c=-\\epsilon_c$), so the common-supercharge failure was a property of pairwise semicircles, not of semicircles glued at a common reference. Reading the source's Appendix B, $X^H_3$ is odd under the through-defect reflection, so the full pairing path is not BPS under a single charge — as a norm should not be. Nothing about primes, contact or poles follows, and the note says so.\n\n**Left undone**, listed in `notes/CONTINUATION_20260918_CLAUDE_SESSION2.md`: the OS-positivity audit against DeWolfe–Freedman–Ooguri (the decisive hypothesis), the defect endpoint conditions for the kets, the cross-arc one-loop term, and registering the new check in `validation/endpoint_matter.py` (I updated the indexes and CHANGELOG but did not re-record the provenance file). Two stray unverified draft files from earlier in the session were deleted rather than shipped. The `Yoshida.pdf` in the project is third-party material and should not be committed, but it is directly relevant to the frame-bound notes."
            ]
          },
